stringx (1.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * -d xor takes a key, so a known key can be read straight off instead of
    brute-forced: -d xor:5a, -d xor:0x5a, -d xor:deadbeef (multi-byte keys
    repeat over the input), or -d xor:s:mykey for a text key. A value that
    isn't valid hex is taken as text.
  * Keys are repeatable (-d xor:5a,xor:deadbeef) and additive: the plain,
    un-XORed input is always scanned as well. A bare -d xor still tries all
    255 single-byte keys. Results are labelled with the key, e.g.
    file->xor(0xdeadbeef).
  * XOR keys apply to -C/--context and --section too: the hexdump shows the
    deciphered bytes, in the right key phase.
  * Reject a key given to a decoder that takes none, e.g. -d hex:5a.

 -- David Araújo <stringx@davidjosearaujo.dev>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:02:11 +0000

stringx (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New --find presets: ipv6, domain, guid, hash_sha1, aws_key,
    github_token, slack_token, google_api_key, jwt, private_key, and
    "secrets" combining all token/key presets. ip now rejects octets > 255.
  * -f, -r, -x and -w are repeatable; filters of the same kind are
    alternatives, different kinds must all match.
  * New decoders: base32, url (%XX), gzip and zlib (whole input or any
    decoded blob), and xor (brute-force all single-byte keys).
  * Decode base64/base32/hex embedded in longer strings ("key=...",
    "0x...", "powershell -enc ..."), and detect UTF-16 in decoded data.
  * New encodings: utf-8 (non-ASCII text) and "all"; -e takes a list.
  * -R/--recursive scans directories; -j/--jobs scans in parallel with
    output kept in input order. "-" reads standard input.
  * -t/--radix prints offsets like strings -t; --color highlights matches;
    -C/--context shows a hexdump around each hit (also in --json).
  * --section now also works on piped input.
  * JSON results include the encoding; length counts characters.
  * Faster text output.
  * build.sh: checksums.txt lists bare file names, so it verifies next to
    downloaded release assets.

 -- David Araújo <stringx@davidjosearaujo.dev>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 15:20:47 +0000

stringx (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix strings that run to the end of the input being dropped, which also
    broke recursive decoding (-d).
  * Decode found strings regardless of output filters, so e.g.
    `-d base64 -f url` finds URLs inside base64 blobs.
  * Count decoded strings in --count mode.
  * Accept URL-safe base64 (-_ alphabet, optional padding) in -d base64.
  * Rename --sector to --section; --sector is still accepted.
  * Report file offsets (not section offsets) with --section, and support
    universal (fat) Mach-O binaries.
  * Replace the wordlist regex with a hash lookup; large wordlists such as
    rockyou.txt are now practical.
  * Validate --length, --encoding, --decode and --count up front.
  * Report the real error for unreadable inputs and exit with status 1.
  * Pager: fix terminal height detection, read keys from the terminal when
    data is piped on stdin, handle very long lines, quit on Ctrl-C.
  * Add --json output for --count; buffer stdout for faster output.
  * Add a test suite.
  * debian/copyright: declare Apache-2.0, matching upstream LICENSE and
    source headers (was incorrectly GPL-3.0+).

 -- David Araújo <stringx@davidjosearaujo.dev>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 14:33:55 +0000

